      "html": "<h3>How often must a care home test its fire alarm?</h3><p>Weekly is standard practice. Test a different call point each week, following BS 5839. The Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005 says the alarm must be maintained. Your fire risk assessment may ask for more.</p><h3>How often is a LOLER inspection needed on a hoist?</h3><p>At least every 6 months. Under LOLER 1998, equipment that lifts people, such as a care home hoist and its slings, needs a thorough examination by a competent person at least every six months. This is a fixed legal minimum.</p><h3>How often must a school do a fire drill?</h3><p>Once a term is the usual rhythm, so three a year. The Fire Safety Order says people must be able to get out safely and that this is practised. Termly is school practice, not a stated legal number.</p><h3>How often does a commercial vehicle need a walkaround check?</h3><p>Every day, before first use. DVSA expects a recorded daily walkaround check of a goods vehicle or PSV. Report and record any defects before the vehicle goes out. This is separate from the annual MOT and the operator's safety inspection.</p><h3>Is PAT testing a legal requirement every year?</h3><p>No. PAT has no fixed legal interval. The Electricity at Work Regulations 1989 say electrical equipment must be kept safe. Set the interval by risk for each type of appliance. Annual is common in care homes. It is not a legal minimum.</p><h3>Are these frequencies fixed by law?</h3><p>Mostly not. A few are fixed, such as the six-monthly LOLER hoist examination and the annual MOT. Most come from your own written risk assessment. Check each item against the named regulator's current guidance.</p>"
