  "seoBody": "<p>This is a plain reference for how often each regular UK check is <strong>usually</strong> done, by sector, with the regulator named.</p><p>Where the law fixes a period, we say so. Where your risk assessment sets it, which is most of them, we say that instead of inventing a number. Check the named regulator's current guidance before you set a schedule.</p><div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>Most UK check frequencies come from risk assessment, not a fixed law. The regulator sets the outcome. Your risk assessment sets the interval.</li><li>A few are fixed: LOLER examination of a hoist is at least every 6 months, the MOT is yearly, and the DVSA walkaround is before first use each day.</li><li>Weekly fire alarm test, monthly and yearly emergency lighting, and yearly extinguisher service are long-standing practice under British Standards.</li><li>PAT has no fixed legal interval. Set it by risk.</li><li>Food and care frequencies mostly sit in your HACCP plan or care risk assessments.</li><li>Use this page as a start. Check every item against the regulator's current guidance.</li></ul></div><h2>Care homes</h2><p>The CQC regulates care in England, the Care Inspectorate in Scotland, CIW in Wales and RQIA in Northern Ireland. HSE, fire and LOLER duties apply on top.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Typical frequency</th><th>Governing body / basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fire alarm test</td><td>Weekly (rotating call point)</td><td>RRO 2005 / BS 5839</td></tr><tr><td>Fire drill</td><td>Commonly every 6 months, more for night staff</td><td>RRO 2005</td></tr><tr><td>Fire door checks</td><td>Commonly every 6 months (quarterly in higher-risk)</td><td>RRO 2005 / Fire Safety (England) Regs</td></tr><tr><td>Legionella / water temperatures</td><td>Commonly monthly outlet temps; regime set by water risk assessment</td><td>HSE HSG274 / ACoP L8</td></tr><tr><td>LOLER hoist thorough examination</td><td>At least every 6 months (fixed)</td><td>LOLER 1998</td></tr><tr><td>PAT</td><td>Commonly annual; risk-assessed, no fixed interval</td><td>Electricity at Work Regs 1989</td></tr><tr><td>IPC (infection prevention) audit</td><td>Commonly monthly to quarterly</td><td>CQC / Care Inspectorate expectation</td></tr><tr><td>Medication (MAR) audit</td><td>Commonly monthly</td><td>CQC / NICE good practice</td></tr><tr><td>Health and safety walk</td><td>Commonly monthly</td><td>HSWA 1974 duty</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Care frequencies for IPC, medication and H&S walks are provider policy set to meet the regulator's expectations, not a single legal number. Confirm against your regulator's current guidance.</p><h2>Scotland: the Care Inspectorate</h2><p>In Scotland the Care Inspectorate regulates care, not the CQC. It has no fixed inspection interval. It inspects on a risk basis, without notice. So you prepare all the time, not to a date. For most \"how often\" questions here, the answer is \"all the time, with evidence on the day\".</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto\"><table><thead><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Typical frequency</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-evaluation against the quality framework</td><td>Continuous, an ongoing expectation (not a once-a-year form)</td><td>Care Inspectorate quality framework and Health and Social Care Standards</td></tr><tr><td>Annual return to the Care Inspectorate</td><td>Annually, in the window the Care Inspectorate sets</td><td>Care Inspectorate registration duty</td></tr><tr><td>Inspection</td><td>Risk-based and unannounced, no fixed interval</td><td>Care Inspectorate scrutiny model</td></tr><tr><td>Notifiable events</td><td>As they occur, within the required timescale</td><td>Care Inspectorate notifiable events guidance</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Frequencies are indicative and the Care Inspectorate updates its guidance; always work from the current published requirements. Quickler is not affiliated with or endorsed by the Care Inspectorate. See our <a href=\"/pages/care-inspectorate-compliance-software-scotland.html\">Care Inspectorate compliance software</a> guide for how a Scottish service captures self-evaluation and evidence on the day.</p><h2>Schools and education estates</h2><p>Schools follow DfE good estate management guidance, HSE duties, and the same fire, legionella and asbestos law as any employer.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Typical frequency</th><th>Governing body / basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fire evacuation drill</td><td>Termly (three a year)</td><td>RRO 2005 / DfE practice</td></tr><tr><td>Fire risk assessment review</td><td>Commonly at least annually, and on material change</td><td>RRO 2005</td></tr><tr><td>Legionella monitoring</td><td>Commonly monthly temps; regime by water risk assessment</td><td>HSE HSG274 / ACoP L8</td></tr><tr><td>Asbestos management check</td><td>Re-inspection of known ACMs at plan interval, commonly at least annual</td><td>Control of Asbestos Regs 2012</td></tr><tr><td>PAT</td><td>Commonly annual; risk-assessed</td><td>Electricity at Work Regs 1989</td></tr><tr><td>Playground / PE equipment</td><td>Annual competent-person inspection plus routine visual checks</td><td>RPII / BS EN 1176 practice</td></tr><tr><td>Emergency lighting</td><td>Monthly function test plus annual full test</td><td>BS 5266 / RRO 2005</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Termly fire drills and annual playground inspections are established practice rather than a single stated legal number. Confirm with your local authority or trust policy.</p><h2>Fleet and transport</h2><p>Operators follow the DVSA Guide to Maintaining Roadworthiness. Walkaround and MOT are firm. Safety inspection intervals are set by the operator on the licence.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Typical frequency</th><th>Governing body / basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Driver walkaround check</td><td>Daily, before first use (fixed expectation)</td><td>DVSA</td></tr><tr><td>Safety inspection</td><td>Operator-set interval, commonly every 4 to 13 weeks</td><td>DVSA / operator licence</td></tr><tr><td>MOT / annual test</td><td>Annual (fixed)</td><td>DVSA</td></tr><tr><td>Tachograph download</td><td>Driver card commonly every 28 days; vehicle unit at least every 90 days</td><td>EU / GB drivers hours rules</td></tr><tr><td>Tachograph calibration</td><td>Every 2 years</td><td>DVSA / approved centre</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The safety-inspection frequency is chosen by the operator from vehicle age, type and use, then declared on the operator licence. It is not a single national figure.</p><h2>Food businesses</h2><p>Food businesses run a HACCP-based food safety system, often the FSA Safer Food, Better Business pack. The local authority EHO inspects and rates them.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Typical frequency</th><th>Governing body / basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Temperature logs</td><td>At least daily; cooking / cooling at each service</td><td>FSA / HACCP plan</td></tr><tr><td>Cleaning schedule</td><td>Daily, with periodic deep cleans</td><td>FSA / HACCP plan</td></tr><tr><td>HACCP review</td><td>On any change; commonly at least annual</td><td>Regulation (EC) 852/2004</td></tr><tr><td>Allergen check</td><td>Ongoing; accurate for every dish</td><td>FSA / Natasha law</td></tr><tr><td>Pest control</td><td>Continuous monitoring; contractor visits at risk-assessed interval</td><td>FSA / HACCP plan</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Most food frequencies are set inside your own HACCP plan rather than by a fixed national number. The EHO checks that your plan exists, is followed and is recorded.</p><h2>General workplace</h2><p>Basic HSE, fire and electrical duties for every UK employer. They sit on top of any sector list above.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Typical frequency</th><th>Governing body / basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fire extinguisher service</td><td>Annual basic service plus monthly visual check</td><td>BS 5306 / RRO 2005</td></tr><tr><td>Emergency lighting</td><td>Monthly function test plus annual full-duration test</td><td>BS 5266 / RRO 2005</td></tr><tr><td>Fire alarm test</td><td>Weekly (rotating call point)</td><td>BS 5839 / RRO 2005</td></tr><tr><td>PAT</td><td>Risk-assessed; no fixed legal interval</td><td>Electricity at Work Regs 1989</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed wiring (EICR)</td><td>Commonly up to every 5 years for many workplaces</td><td>Electricity at Work Regs 1989 / BS 7671</td></tr><tr><td>First aid provision</td><td>Needs assessed; kit commonly checked monthly</td><td>Health and Safety (First-Aid) Regs 1981</td></tr><tr><td>RIDDOR reporting</td><td>Not a schedule; report reportable events when they occur</td><td>RIDDOR 2013 / HSE</td></tr></tbody></table><p>RIDDOR is a reporting duty, not a recurring check. Specified injuries, occupational diseases and dangerous occurrences must be reported to the HSE within the RIDDOR time limits.</p><h2>Record the check on the day</h2><p>Knowing the frequency is half the job. The other half is a dated record that the check happened. Make it at the time, not later from memory.</p><p>Quickler can schedule any regular check above in WhatsApp. On the due day the person on the job gets the questions and answers them, with a photo where it helps. You get a dated, photographed record and a web link to share. No app. No login. Pricing is [[price:bundles]], after a 14-day free trial. See the <a href=\"/pages/demo.html\">demo</a>.</p><h2>Questions, answered</h2><h3>How often must a care home test its fire alarm?</h3><p>Weekly is standard practice. Test a different call point each week, following BS 5839. The Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005 says the alarm must be maintained. Your fire risk assessment may ask for more.</p><h3>How often is a LOLER inspection needed on a hoist?</h3><p>At least every 6 months. Under LOLER 1998, equipment that lifts people, such as a care home hoist and its slings, needs a thorough examination by a competent person at least every six months. This is a fixed legal minimum.</p><h3>How often must a school do a fire drill?</h3><p>Once a term is the usual rhythm, so three a year. The Fire Safety Order says people must be able to get out safely and that this is practised. Termly is school practice, not a stated legal number.</p><h3>How often does a commercial vehicle need a walkaround check?</h3><p>Every day, before first use. DVSA expects a recorded daily walkaround check of a goods vehicle or PSV. Report and record any defects before the vehicle goes out. This is separate from the annual MOT and the operator's safety inspection.</p><h3>Is PAT testing a legal requirement every year?</h3><p>No. PAT has no fixed legal interval. The Electricity at Work Regulations 1989 say electrical equipment must be kept safe. Set the interval by risk for each type of appliance. Annual is common in care homes. It is not a legal minimum.</p><h3>Are these frequencies fixed by law?</h3><p>Mostly not. A few are fixed, such as the six-monthly LOLER hoist examination and the annual MOT. Most come from your own written risk assessment. Check each item against the named regulator's current guidance.</p>",
